To optimize your chatbot application for troubleshooting mobile phone calling issues, you need a strategy to determine when to perform a vector search in your Mongo Atlas vector store versus when to rely on the LLM’s existing context or general knowledge. The goal is to minimize latency while ensuring the chatbot provides accurate, contextually relevant responses. Below are some strategies to help you decide when to trigger a vector search:

1. Use Intent and Entity Detection to Trigger Vector Search
	•	How it works: At the start of the session, you already know the high-level issue (e.g., “trouble making calls”). As the conversation progresses, use natural language processing (NLP) to detect specific intents or entities (e.g., “Samsung Galaxy S22,” “LG Velvet,” “5G network”) that indicate a need for detailed, model-specific information.
	•	When to trigger vector search: Perform a vector search only when a specific entity (e.g., phone model, network type) is mentioned that requires detailed troubleshooting steps or data not likely to be in the LLM’s general knowledge.
	•	Implementation: Use an NLP library (e.g., spaCy, Rasa, or a custom entity recognition model) to extract entities from user input. If an entity matches a predefined list of “specificity triggers” (e.g., phone models, OS versions), initiate a vector search.
	•	Example:
	◦	User: “I can’t make calls.”
	▪	No vector search (general troubleshooting applies).
	◦	User: “I can’t make calls on my Samsung Galaxy S23.”
	▪	Trigger vector search for “Samsung Galaxy S23” troubleshooting data.

2. Leverage Session Context and State Management
	•	How it works: Maintain a session state (e.g., in memory or a lightweight database) that tracks the user’s issue and any specific details provided so far. Use this state to decide if a vector search is necessary.
	•	When to trigger vector search: If the session state lacks sufficient detail to answer a question (e.g., no phone model specified yet) and the user provides a new specific detail, trigger a vector search. Avoid redundant searches if the context is already populated.
	•	Implementation: Store key variables like issue_type, phone_model, and network_type in the session. Check these before deciding to search.
	•	Example:
	◦	Session state: {issue: "trouble making calls"}
	◦	User: “It’s my LG phone.”
	◦	Update state: {issue: "trouble making calls", phone_model: "LG"}
	◦	Trigger vector search for LG-specific troubleshooting.

3. Predefine General vs. Specific Knowledge Thresholds
	•	How it works: Categorize troubleshooting knowledge into “general” (e.g., restart phone, check signal) and “specific” (e.g., firmware bug in Samsung Galaxy A52). Let the LLM handle general queries and reserve vector search for specific cases.
	•	When to trigger vector search: If the user’s input crosses into “specific” territory (e.g., mentions a model, error code, or carrier), perform a vector search. Otherwise, let the LLM respond with general advice.
	•	Implementation: Create a rule-based or machine-learned classifier to distinguish general vs. specific queries based on keywords or patterns.
	•	Example:
	◦	User: “Why do my calls keep dropping?”
	▪	General response from LLM: “Check your signal strength or restart your phone.”
	◦	User: “It’s dropping on my iPhone 14 with Verizon.”
	▪	Trigger vector search for “iPhone 14 Verizon dropped calls.”

4. Cache Frequently Accessed Vector Search Results
	•	How it works: Store the results of common vector searches (e.g., popular phone models like iPhone 13 or Samsung Galaxy S21) in a fast-access cache (e.g., Redis). This reduces latency for repeated queries.
	•	When to trigger vector search: Check the cache first. If the data isn’t cached and the query requires specific info, perform a vector search and cache the result.
	•	Implementation: Use a key-value store with a TTL (time-to-live) to keep the cache fresh. Key could be "{phone_model}_{issue_type}".
	•	Example:
	◦	User: “Trouble making calls on Galaxy S22.”
	◦	Check cache → If found, use cached result; if not, vector search and cache it.

5. Use Confidence Scoring from the LLM
	•	How it works: Let the LLM evaluate its own confidence in answering a query based on the current context. If the confidence is low (e.g., due to lack of specific details), trigger a vector search.
	•	When to trigger vector search: Set a confidence threshold (e.g., 0.7). If the LLM’s confidence falls below this, perform a vector search to fetch relevant data.
	•	Implementation: Fine-tune your LLM to output a confidence score or use a secondary model to assess response quality.
	•	Example:
	◦	User: “Calls aren’t working.”
	▪	LLM confidence: 0.9 (general response: “Restart your phone”).
	◦	User: “It’s my Nokia G50.”
	▪	LLM confidence: 0.4 (triggers vector search for Nokia G50 issues).

6. Progressive Specificity Detection
	•	How it works: Start with broad, low-latency responses and progressively narrow down as the user provides more details. Only trigger vector search when the conversation reaches a point requiring precise, model-specific info.
	•	When to trigger vector search: Wait until the user explicitly or implicitly signals a need for deeper info (e.g., “That didn’t work” or “It’s a Huawei phone”).
	•	Implementation: Design a conversation flow with stages (e.g., initial diagnosis → model-specific steps). Move to vector search at later stages.
	•	Example:
	◦	User: “I can’t get calls.”
	▪	LLM: “Check if airplane mode is off.”
	◦	User: “It’s off, but my OnePlus 9 still doesn’t work.”
	▪	Trigger vector search for “OnePlus 9 incoming call issues.”

7. Hybrid Approach: Rules + Machine Learning
	•	How it works: Combine rule-based triggers (e.g., keyword detection) with a machine learning model trained to predict when a vector search will improve response quality.
	•	When to trigger vector search: Use rules for obvious cases (e.g., phone model mentioned) and the ML model for ambiguous cases (e.g., “It’s a new phone” → predict if search is needed).
	•	Implementation: Train a small classifier on historical chat data, labeling instances where vector search was necessary vs. not.
	•	Example:
	◦	Rule: “Samsung” → Vector search.
	◦	ML: “It’s dropping all the time” → Model predicts if specific context is needed.

Practical Tips to Reduce Latency
	•	Batch Vector Searches: If multiple pieces of info might be needed (e.g., model + carrier), batch them into one search.
	•	Optimize Vector Store: Index your Mongo Atlas vector store efficiently and limit search scope (e.g., filter by issue type).
	•	Asynchronous Search: Run vector searches in the background and provide a general response first, then follow up with specifics if needed.

Recommended Strategy
A hybrid approach works best for your use case:
	1	Start with intent/entity detection to catch obvious triggers (e.g., phone models).
	2	Use session state to avoid redundant searches.
	3	Add caching for popular queries to minimize latency.
	4	Fall back to confidence scoring or progressive specificity for edge cases.
This balances speed and accuracy, ensuring vector searches happen only when necessary. Test with real user sessions to fine-tune thresholds and rules!
